{"nbformat":4,"nbformat_minor":0,"metadata":{"colab":{"provenance":[{"file_id":"1dAw-FVO1uxKnVvUDFfWWGHPBXAy0v40z","timestamp":1758633832423}]},"kernelspec":{"name":"python3","display_name":"Python 3"},"language_info":{"name":"python"}},"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# QUESTION 1 -- Working with Binary"],"metadata":{"id":"18xMgo9holX2"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## Part I: Decimal --> binary"],"metadata":{"id":"ch5zIAnxq8Vi"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Below is code that converts from decimal to binary. Test it on a few cases to convince yourself that it works. (It basically just executes the procedure we covered in class). In this exercise, you're going to deconstruct the code and explain some of its various components. Then, you'll synthesize these into a final comprehensive explanation."],"metadata":{"id":"IRaZfrvWlzJy"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"RiFYXxMdyNy_","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1759285439209,"user_tz":240,"elapsed":8,"user":{"displayName":"Benjamin Adovasio","userId":"01112592304871986613"}},"outputId":"186472b4-f308-4dae-f16b-8d05a6ef944a"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["the binary string for 431 is 110101111\n"]}],"source":["import math\n","\n","decimal_value = 431 # try some different values here\n","remaining_value = decimal_value\n","binary_string = '' # the final answer, which we'll build iteratively below\n","\n","# number of digits/'buckets' we'll need for our binary digit\n","num_digits = 1 + math.floor(math.log2(decimal_value))\n","\n","# iterate through the buckets\n","for i in list(range(num_digits))[::-1]:\n","  base = 2 ** i # 2 to the i-th power\n","  if remaining_value >= base:\n","    remaining_value -= base\n","    binary_string = binary_string + '1'\n","  else:\n","    binary_string = binary_string + '0'\n","\n","print(f'the binary string for {decimal_value} is {binary_string}')\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## 1a"],"metadata":{"id":"Aa2bihAPzpIB"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**STARTER QUESTIONS:**\n","\n","- What is the purpose of the variable `remaining_value`? (In addition to answering below, insert a 'print' statement that helps you explore the behavior of `remaining_value` as the loop runs).\n","- Why is `binary_string` initialized to the empty string to start?\n","\n","<br>\n","\n","**YOUR ANSWERS:**\n","- The purpose of `remaining_value` is to keep track of how much is left before it gets to 0, allowing us to iterate through the correct number of times.\n","- We are building the binary string as we iterate through, so it needs ot start as nothing.\n"],"metadata":{"id":"aUprPS07mn-Z"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## 1b"],"metadata":{"id":"Rja2VR3pzwwM"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**QUESTION:** In the \"sandbox\" below, do some experiments to test what `list(range(num_digits))[::-1]` does. You'll want to invent your own data to test things on, and use `print` statements to explore and test behavior. **Break the expression up into small pieces that you test** -- don't just try to explain it in one shot. Use comments to explain conclusions/discoveries that you arrive at."],"metadata":{"id":"Eu9cPZP0ncp9"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["# 'Sandbox' for you to experiment with small pieces of the code above, and test their behavior.\n","\n","print(num_digits) #We have 9 digits\n","\n","print(list(range(num_digits))[::-1]) #It starts at 8 because 0 counts as a digit in coding\n","\n","print(list(range(num_digits))[1]) #Returns 1\n","\n","print(list(range(num_digits))[3]) #Returns 3 (not sure why)\n","\n","print(list(range(num_digits))[::3]) #Returns first 3 items in the list\n","\n","print(list(range(num_digits))[::-3]) #Returns last 3 items in the list"],"metadata":{"id":"oaaTMxZplYA1","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1759282107092,"user_tz":240,"elapsed":6,"user":{"displayName":"Benjamin Adovasio","userId":"01112592304871986613"}},"outputId":"7f8e1275-966a-4b2c-bb5e-b5a51df512d6"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["9\n","[8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0]\n","1\n","3\n","[0, 3, 6]\n","[8, 5, 2]\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## 1c"],"metadata":{"id":"pj0a8Qg_z0Of"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**QUESTION:** Explain, in the clearest language you can, how the decimal conversion code above works. Provide a line-by-line \"walkthrough\", explaining the logic and providing examples as needed. It may take quite a few words to explain this accurately!\n","\n","**ANSWER:**\n","\n","The first thing that happens is the code copies the value of whatever is set as \"decimal_value\" to \"remaining_value\" so we can safely modify it without changing the original value. The next line is fairly simple, it just creates an empty string to build the binary value from. Next we do some math to calculate how many digits we even need. Breaking down the expression num_digits=1+math.floor(math.log2(decimal_value)) we get the log base 2 of decimal_value, rounded, and then plus one. We need to add one because in coding numbers start at 0. We then iterate through each digit in the for loop, backwards, stopping at 0. If the remaining value is greater than or equal to base, then we subtract base from it and add 1 to binary_string. Otherwise we add a 0 to the binary string. After all the iterations, we print out the final binary string as well as how many total digits.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"ALxXp0UYoKaK"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## Part II: Binary --> Decimal"],"metadata":{"id":"Q3CwQwghrHSd"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Below is skeleton code that converts a binary string to its corresponding decimal value. Fill in the missing details so that it correctly prints a value at the end. You should only attempt this once you have a good understanding of how the code above works. This is definitely tough, but definitely do-able! **This will form the basis of an extra-credit question on the next quiz** (a regular question would probably be a bit mean...)"],"metadata":{"id":"lF4YGeyTrMlg"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["## 1d"],"metadata":{"id":"3cN9chPXz86V"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["binary_string = '11011' # experiment with other values!\n","decimal_value = 0 #initialize to zero\n","\n","# iterate through the indices of the binary string\n","for i in list(range(len(binary_string))):\n","\n","  # look at what this print statement outputs -- you'll find it handy for your procedure:\n","  print(len(binary_string) - i - 1)\n","\n","  # add your code here for computing the decimal value. Look carefully at your class notes!\n","  # some code hints are below:\n","\n","  # Hint 1: set up an iteration (loop) over string elements (already done for you above)\n","\n","  # Hint 2: Inside the loop, have a conditional statement that evaluates one way\n","  # IF the character encountered is a '1', and another way (ELSE) if the character\n","  # encountered is a '0'.\n","\n","  # Hint 3: Think about how you'd need 'decimal_value' to change in each of the IF/ELSE\n","  # conditions above. It will involve an expression with 'i' -- the index you're 'walking\n","  # along', but you'll need to be a bit clever. The print statement above should be *very* useful ;-)\n","\n","  if binary_string[i] == '1':\n","    decimal_value=decimal_value+2** (len(binary_string)-i-1)\n","    print(\"Its a one!\")\n","  else:\n","    print(\"Not doing anything because its a zero\")\n","\n","\n","# This procedure should print the final decimal value.\n","print('result:'+ str(decimal_value))"],"metadata":{"id":"3Vf6b-kHypus","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1759285314888,"user_tz":240,"elapsed":8,"user":{"displayName":"Benjamin Adovasio","userId":"01112592304871986613"}},"outputId":"edaafabc-17e8-40